{"slug":"/students/subjects/chemistry/foundations/symbols-formulas/","source":"students/subjects/chemistry/foundations/symbols-formulas.html","area":"students","kind":"topic","title":"Symbols & Formula Writing | Chemistry Foundations","description":"","subject":"chemistry","body":"<div class=\"max-w-4xl mx-auto\">\n    <!-- Navigation -->\n    <nav class=\"mb-4 flex items-center justify-between\">\n      <a href=\"/students/subjects/chemistry/foundations/\" class=\"text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 transition flex items-center\">\n        <span class=\"mi -arrow-left mr-2\" data-mi=\"arrow_back\">arrow_back</span>Back to Foundations\n      </a>\n      <div class=\"flex items-center gap-3\">\n        <div class=\"text-sm text-gray-600\">\n          <span id=\"topic-progress-text\" data-bidemy=\"progress-text\">0% Complete</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"w-24 h-2 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden\" data-bidemy=\"progress\">\n          <div id=\"topic-progress-bar\" class=\"h-full bg-gradient-to-r from-violet-400 to-purple-500 transition-all duration-300\" style=\"width: 0%\"></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </nav>\n\n    <article class=\"content-card rounded-xl p-6 md:p-8 shadow-lg\">\n      <!-- Header -->\n      <div class=\"border-b pb-4 mb-6\">\n        <h1 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2\">\n          <span class=\"mi -font text-violet-500 mr-3\" data-mi=\"help_outline\">help_outline</span>Symbols &amp; Formula Writing\n        </h1>\n        <p class=\"text-gray-600\">Understanding chemical symbols, valency, and writing formulas for ionic and covalent compounds.</p>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- Learning Objectives -->\n      <div class=\"bg-violet-50 border-l-4 border-violet-500 p-4 rounded-r-lg mb-6\">\n        <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-violet-800 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -bullseye mr-2\" data-mi=\"adjust\">adjust</span>Learning Objectives</h3>\n        <ul class=\"text-violet-700 text-sm space-y-1\">\n          <li>• State chemical symbols of common elements</li>\n          <li>• Define valency and know valencies of common elements</li>\n          <li>• Write correct formulas for simple ionic compounds</li>\n          <li>• Write formulas for covalent molecules</li>\n          <li>• Use brackets in formula writing correctly</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- Section 1: Chemical Symbols -->\n      <section id=\"section-1\" class=\"mb-8\">\n        <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n          <span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">1</span>\n          Chemical Symbols\n        </h2>\n\n        <div class=\"bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-lg mb-4\">\n          <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">A <strong class=\"text-violet-600\">chemical symbol</strong> is a shorthand way of representing an element. Most symbols consist of one or two letters from the element's name.</p>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-gradient-to-r from-violet-100 to-purple-100 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4\">\n            <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-violet-800 mb-2\">Rules for Writing Symbols:</h4>\n            <ul class=\"text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1\">\n              <li>• First letter is always <strong>CAPITAL</strong></li>\n              <li>• Second letter (if any) is always <strong>lowercase</strong></li>\n              <li>• Examples: <strong>C</strong>arbon, <strong>H</strong>ydrogen, <strong>Na</strong>trium (Sodium), <strong>Fe</strong>rrum (Iron)</li>\n            </ul>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"overflow-x-auto\">\n            <table class=\"w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-300 rounded-lg overflow-hidden\">\n              <thead class=\"bg-violet-600 text-white\">\n                <tr>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Element</th>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Symbol</th>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Element</th>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Symbol</th>\n                </tr>\n              </thead>\n              <tbody class=\"bg-white\">\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Hydrogen</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">H</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Oxygen</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">O</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Carbon</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">C</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Nitrogen</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">N</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Sodium</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">Na</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Potassium</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">K</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Iron</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">Fe</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Copper</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">Cu</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Calcium</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">Ca</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Magnesium</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">Mg</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Chlorine</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">Cl</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Sulfur</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">S</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Zinc</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">Zn</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Aluminum</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold text-violet-700\">Al</td>\n                </tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 border-l-4 border-yellow-400 p-4 rounded-r-lg mt-4\">\n            <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-yellow-800 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -info-circle mr-2\" data-mi=\"info\">info</span>Latin Names</h4>\n            <p class=\"text-gray-700 text-sm\">Some symbols come from Latin names: <strong>Na</strong> (Natrium = Sodium), <strong>K</strong> (Kalium = Potassium), <strong>Fe</strong> (Ferrum = Iron), <strong>Cu</strong> (Cuprum = Copper), <strong>Ag</strong> (Argentum = Silver), <strong>Au</strong> (Aurum = Gold), <strong>Pb</strong> (Plumbum = Lead).</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- Section 2: Valency -->\n      <section id=\"section-2\" class=\"mb-8\">\n        <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n          <span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">2</span>\n          Valency\n        </h2>\n\n        <div class=\"bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-lg mb-4\">\n          <div class=\"bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-100 to-cyan-100 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4 border-l-4 border-blue-500\">\n            <p class=\"text-lg font-semibold text-blue-800 mb-1\">Definition</p>\n            <p class=\"text-gray-700\"><strong class=\"text-blue-600\">Valency</strong> is the combining power of an element - the number of electrons it can lose, gain, or share to form compounds.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"overflow-x-auto\">\n            <table class=\"w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-300 rounded-lg overflow-hidden\">\n              <thead class=\"bg-blue-600 text-white\">\n                <tr>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Element</th>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Symbol</th>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Valency</th>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Group</th>\n                </tr>\n              </thead>\n              <tbody class=\"bg-white\">\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Hydrogen</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">H</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-bold text-blue-700\">1</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">1</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Sodium, Potassium</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">Na, K</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-bold text-blue-700\">1</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">1</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Magnesium, Calcium</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">Mg, Ca</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-bold text-green-700\">2</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">2</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Aluminum</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">Al</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-bold text-orange-700\">3</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">3</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Carbon, Silicon</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">C, Si</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-bold text-purple-700\">4</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">4</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Nitrogen, Phosphorus</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">N, P</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-bold text-red-700\">3</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">5</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Oxygen, Sulfur</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">O, S</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-bold text-green-700\">2</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">6</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Chlorine, Fluorine</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">Cl, F</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-bold text-blue-700\">1</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">7</td>\n                </tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 border-l-4 border-yellow-400 p-4 rounded-r-lg\">\n          <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-yellow-800 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -lightbulb mr-2\" data-mi=\"lightbulb\">lightbulb</span>Variable Valency</h4>\n          <p class=\"text-gray-700 text-sm\">Some elements have more than one valency:</p>\n          <ul class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1 space-y-1\">\n            <li>• <strong>Iron (Fe):</strong> Fe²⁺ (ferrous) or Fe³⁺ (ferric)</li>\n            <li>• <strong>Copper (Cu):</strong> Cu⁺ (cuprous) or Cu²⁺ (cupric)</li>\n            <li>• <strong>Lead (Pb):</strong> Pb²⁺ or Pb⁴⁺</li>\n          </ul>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- Section 3: Writing Ionic Formulas -->\n      <section id=\"section-3\" class=\"mb-8\">\n        <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n          <span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">3</span>\n          Writing Formulas for Ionic Compounds\n        </h2>\n\n        <div class=\"bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-lg mb-4\">\n          <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">Ionic compounds form when metals transfer electrons to non-metals. The formula shows the simplest ratio of ions.</p>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 mb-4\">\n            <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3\">Steps for Writing Ionic Formulas:</h4>\n            <ol class=\"text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-2\">\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-6 h-6 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold flex-shrink-0\">1</span> Write the symbols of the ions (metal first)</li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-6 h-6 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold flex-shrink-0\">2</span> Write their valencies (charges)</li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-6 h-6 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold flex-shrink-0\">3</span> Cross over the valencies (swap them)</li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-6 h-6 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold flex-shrink-0\">4</span> Simplify if needed</li>\n            </ol>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-green-200\">\n            <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-green-800 mb-3\">The \"Swap and Drop\" Method:</h4>\n            <div class=\"flex items-center justify-center gap-4 text-lg\">\n              <div class=\"text-center\">\n                <p class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-green-700\">Na⁺</p>\n                <p class=\"text-xs text-gray-500\">valency 1</p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"text-2xl text-gray-400\">+</div>\n              <div class=\"text-center\">\n                <p class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-red-700\">Cl⁻</p>\n                <p class=\"text-xs text-gray-500\">valency 1</p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"text-2xl text-gray-400\">→</div>\n              <div class=\"text-center\">\n                <p class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-violet-700\">NaCl</p>\n                <p class=\"text-xs text-gray-500\">1:1 ratio</p>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Worked Examples -->\n        <div class=\"border border-gray-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden\">\n          <button class=\"accordion-btn w-full p-4 bg-violet-50 text-left font-semibold text-violet-800 flex justify-between items-center hover:bg-violet-100 transition\">\n            <span><span class=\"mi -calculator mr-2 text-violet-500\" data-mi=\"calculate\">calculate</span>Worked Examples: Ionic Formulas</span>\n            <span class=\"mi -chevron-down transform transition-transform\" data-mi=\"expand_more\">expand_more</span>\n          </button>\n          <div class=\"accordion-content\">\n            <div class=\"p-5 space-y-4\">\n              <div class=\"step-card bg-gray-50 p-3 rounded border-l-4 border-blue-500\">\n                <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800\">1. Magnesium + Chlorine</p>\n                <p class=\"text-blue-700 text-sm mt-1\">Mg²⁺ + Cl⁻ → Cross: Mg₁Cl₂ → <strong>MgCl₂</strong></p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"step-card bg-gray-50 p-3 rounded border-l-4 border-green-500\">\n                <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800\">2. Calcium + Oxygen</p>\n                <p class=\"text-green-700 text-sm mt-1\">Ca²⁺ + O²⁻ → Cross: Ca₂O₂ → Simplify → <strong>CaO</strong></p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"step-card bg-gray-50 p-3 rounded border-l-4 border-purple-500\">\n                <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800\">3. Aluminum + Oxygen</p>\n                <p class=\"text-purple-700 text-sm mt-1\">Al³⁺ + O²⁻ → Cross: Al₂O₃ → <strong>Al₂O₃</strong></p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"step-card bg-gray-50 p-3 rounded border-l-4 border-orange-500\">\n                <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800\">4. Sodium + Sulfate</p>\n                <p class=\"text-orange-700 text-sm mt-1\">Na⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → Cross: Na₂(SO₄)₁ → <strong>Na₂SO₄</strong></p>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- Section 4: Polyatomic Ions -->\n      <section id=\"section-4\" class=\"mb-8\">\n        <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n          <span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">4</span>\n          Polyatomic Ions (Radicals)\n        </h2>\n\n        <div class=\"bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-lg mb-4\">\n          <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\"><strong class=\"text-violet-600\">Polyatomic ions</strong> are groups of atoms that carry a charge and act as a single unit. Use brackets when more than one is needed.</p>\n\n          <div class=\"overflow-x-auto\">\n            <table class=\"w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-300 rounded-lg overflow-hidden\">\n              <thead class=\"bg-violet-600 text-white\">\n                <tr>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Name</th>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Formula</th>\n                  <th class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Charge</th>\n                </tr>\n              </thead>\n              <tbody class=\"bg-white\">\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Ammonium</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold\">NH₄⁺</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">+1</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Hydroxide</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold\">OH⁻</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">-1</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Nitrate</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold\">NO₃⁻</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">-1</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Carbonate</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold\">CO₃²⁻</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">-2</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Sulfate</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold\">SO₄²⁻</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">-2</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2\">Phosphate</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center font-mono font-bold\">PO₄³⁻</td>\n                  <td class=\"border border-gray-300 p-2 text-center\">-3</td>\n                </tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 border-l-4 border-yellow-400 p-4 rounded-r-lg mt-4\">\n            <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-yellow-800 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -exclamation-triangle mr-2\" data-mi=\"warning_amber\">warning_amber</span>Important: Using Brackets</h4>\n            <p class=\"text-gray-700 text-sm\">When more than one polyatomic ion is needed, enclose it in brackets:</p>\n            <ul class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1\">\n              <li>• Ca²⁺ + (OH)⁻ → <strong>Ca(OH)₂</strong> ✓</li>\n              <li>• Not CaOH₂ ✗ (wrong!)</li>\n              <li>• Al³⁺ + (SO₄)²⁻ → <strong>Al₂(SO₄)₃</strong></li>\n            </ul>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- Section 5: Covalent Formulas -->\n      <section id=\"section-5\" class=\"mb-8\">\n        <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n          <span class=\"bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">5</span>\n          Formulas for Covalent Compounds\n        </h2>\n\n        <div class=\"bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-lg mb-4\">\n          <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">Covalent compounds form when non-metals share electrons. The formula shows the actual number of atoms in each molecule.</p>\n\n          <div class=\"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200\">\n              <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2\">Common Covalent Molecules</h4>\n              <ul class=\"text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1\">\n                <li>• Water: <strong>H₂O</strong></li>\n                <li>• Carbon dioxide: <strong>CO₂</strong></li>\n                <li>• Methane: <strong>CH₄</strong></li>\n                <li>• Ammonia: <strong>NH₃</strong></li>\n                <li>• Hydrogen chloride: <strong>HCl</strong></li>\n              </ul>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200\">\n              <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2\">Diatomic Elements</h4>\n              <ul class=\"text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1\">\n                <li>• Hydrogen: <strong>H₂</strong></li>\n                <li>• Nitrogen: <strong>N₂</strong></li>\n                <li>• Oxygen: <strong>O₂</strong></li>\n                <li>• Chlorine: <strong>Cl₂</strong></li>\n                <li class=\"text-xs text-gray-500\">Also: F₂, Br₂, I₂</li>\n              </ul>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-purple-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-purple-200\">\n            <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-purple-800 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -lightbulb mr-2\" data-mi=\"lightbulb\">lightbulb</span>Remember</h4>\n            <p class=\"text-gray-700 text-sm\">For covalent compounds, use <strong>prefixes</strong> to indicate numbers: mono (1), di (2), tri (3), tetra (4), penta (5). Example: Carbon <em>di</em>oxide (CO₂), Dinitrogen <em>tri</em>oxide (N₂O₃).</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- Section 6: Practice Problems -->\n      <section id=\"section-6\" class=\"mb-8\">\n        <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n          <span class=\"mi -pencil-alt text-violet-600 mr-3\" data-mi=\"edit\">edit</span>\n          Practice Problems\n        </h2>\n\n        <div class=\"space-y-4\">\n          <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-yellow-200\">\n            <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800 mb-2\">1. Write the formula for: (a) Sodium oxide, (b) Magnesium chloride, (c) Aluminum chloride</p>\n            <details class=\"mt-2\">\n              <summary class=\"text-sm text-blue-600 cursor-pointer hover:text-blue-800\">Show Solution</summary>\n              <div class=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-1\">\n                <p>(a) Na⁺ + O²⁻ → <strong>Na₂O</strong></p>\n                <p>(b) Mg²⁺ + Cl⁻ → <strong>MgCl₂</strong></p>\n                <p>(c) Al³⁺ + Cl⁻ → <strong>AlCl₃</strong></p>\n              </div>\n            </details>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-yellow-200\">\n            <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800 mb-2\">2. Write formulas for: (a) Calcium hydroxide, (b) Aluminum sulfate, (c) Ammonium nitrate</p>\n            <details class=\"mt-2\">\n              <summary class=\"text-sm text-blue-600 cursor-pointer hover:text-blue-800\">Show Solution</summary>\n              <div class=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-1\">\n                <p>(a) Ca²⁺ + OH⁻ → <strong>Ca(OH)₂</strong></p>\n                <p>(b) Al³⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → <strong>Al₂(SO₄)₃</strong></p>\n                <p>(c) NH₄⁺ + NO₃⁻ → <strong>NH₄NO₃</strong></p>\n              </div>\n            </details>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-yellow-200\">\n            <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800 mb-2\">3. Name these compounds: (a) FeO, (b) Fe₂O₃, (c) CuO, (d) Cu₂O</p>\n            <details class=\"mt-2\">\n              <summary class=\"text-sm text-blue-600 cursor-pointer hover:text-blue-800\">Show Solution</summary>\n              <div class=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-1\">\n                <p>(a) FeO → <strong>Iron(II) oxide</strong> (Fe²⁺)</p>\n                <p>(b) Fe₂O₃ → <strong>Iron(III) oxide</strong> (Fe³⁺)</p>\n                <p>(c) CuO → <strong>Copper(II) oxide</strong> (Cu²⁺)</p>\n                <p>(d) Cu₂O → <strong>Copper(I) oxide</strong> (Cu⁺)</p>\n              </div>\n            </details>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-yellow-200\">\n            <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800 mb-2\">4. What is wrong with these formulas? (a) CaOH₂, (b) Na₂Cl</p>\n            <details class=\"mt-2\">\n              <summary class=\"text-sm text-blue-600 cursor-pointer hover:text-blue-800\">Show Solution</summary>\n              <div class=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-1\">\n                <p>(a) CaOH₂ is wrong - should be <strong>Ca(OH)₂</strong> (brackets needed for hydroxide)</p>\n                <p>(b) Na₂Cl is wrong - should be <strong>NaCl</strong> (Na⁺ + Cl⁻ = 1:1 ratio)</p>\n              </div>\n            </details>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- Key Takeaways -->\n      <section id=\"section-7\" class=\"mb-8\">\n        <div class=\"bg-gradient-to-r from-violet-50 to-purple-50 p-5 rounded-lg border border-violet-200\">\n          <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-violet-800 mb-3\"><span class=\"mi -star mr-2\" data-mi=\"star\">star</span>Key Takeaways for WAEC/JAMB/NECO</h3>\n          <ul class=\"text-violet-700 text-sm space-y-2\">\n            <li>• Know symbols of common elements (especially Latin names: Na, K, Fe, Cu, Ag, Au, Pb)</li>\n            <li>• Memorize valencies: Group 1 = 1, Group 2 = 2, Group 3 = 3, etc.</li>\n            <li>• \"Swap and drop\" method: cross valencies, simplify if needed</li>\n            <li>• Use brackets for polyatomic ions when more than one is needed</li>\n            <li>• Know common polyatomic ions: OH⁻, NO₃⁻, CO₃²⁻, SO₄²⁻, NH₄⁺</li>\n            <li>• Variable valency elements: Fe (2,3), Cu (1,2), Pb (2,4)</li>\n          </ul>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- Complete Topic Button -->\n      <div class=\"mt-8 pt-6 border-t border-gray-200\">\n        <div class=\"flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-4\">\n          <div class=\"text-center sm:text-left\">\n            <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-gray-800\">Ready to Complete?</h3>\n            <p class=\"text-sm text-gray-600\">View all sections, then take the quiz to mark this topic as complete.</p>\n          </div>\n          <button id=\"topic-complete-btn\" disabled=\"\" class=\"px-6 py-3 rounded-xl bg-gray-300 text-gray-500 cursor-not-allowed opacity-50 flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-medium\">\n            <span class=\"mi -lock mr-2\" data-mi=\"lock\">lock</span>\n            <span>Complete (0% viewed)</span>\n          </button>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </article>\n  </div>\n\n  \n\n  \n\n\n<div data-bidemy=\"quiz\"></div>","bodyClass":"min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-violet-50 to-purple-100 p-4 md:p-6","bodyStyle":"","css":["body { font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; }\n    .content-card {\n      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(12px);\n      backdrop-filter: blur(12px);\n      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);\n      border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);\n    }\n    .accordion-content {\n      max-height: 0;\n      overflow: hidden;\n      transition: max-height 0.3s ease-out;\n    }\n    .accordion-content.open {\n      max-height: 2000px;\n    }\n    .step-card { transition: all 0.2s ease; }\n    .step-card:hover { transform: translateX(4px); }\n    #topic-complete-btn { transition: all 0.3s ease; }\n    #topic-complete-btn:not(:disabled):hover { transform: scale(1.02); }"],"quiz":{"topicId":"symbols-formulas","subject":"chemistry","passingScore":60,"questions":[{"question":"What is the symbol for potassium?","options":["P","Po","K","Pt"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Potassium comes from Latin 'Kalium', so its symbol is K."},{"question":"The valency of aluminum is:","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Aluminum (Al) is in Group 3 and has valency 3."},{"question":"What is the formula of magnesium oxide?","options":["MgO","MgO₂","Mg₂O","Mg₂O₃"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Mg²⁺ + O²⁻ → MgO (valencies cancel out)."},{"question":"The formula Ca(OH)₂ represents:","options":["Calcium oxide","Calcium hydroxide","Calcium carbonate","Calcium sulfate"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Ca(OH)₂ is calcium hydroxide (lime water)."},{"question":"Why are brackets used in Ca(OH)₂?","options":["To show it's a compound","Because OH is a polyatomic ion and 2 are needed","To make it look nice","It's optional"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Brackets show that the entire hydroxide ion (OH⁻) appears twice."},{"question":"What is the formula of sodium sulfate?","options":["NaSO₄","Na₂SO₄","Na(SO₄)₂","NaSO₄₂"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Na⁺ (valency 1) + SO₄²⁻ (valency 2) → Na₂SO₄."},{"question":"Iron(III) oxide has the formula:","options":["FeO","Fe₂O","Fe₂O₃","Fe₃O₂"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Iron(III) means Fe³⁺. Fe³⁺ + O²⁻ → Fe₂O₃."},{"question":"Which element has variable valency?","options":["Sodium","Magnesium","Iron","Aluminum"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Iron has two valencies: Fe²⁺ (ferrous) and Fe³⁺ (ferric)."}]},"tracker":{"topicId":"symbols-formulas","topicTitle":"Symbols & Formula Writing","subject":"chemistry","sections":["section-1","section-2","section-3","section-4","section-5","section-6","section-7"],"quizRequired":true},"links":[]}